{"id":"4579379a-1e20-42d6-99bd-5a0374d08615","object":"chat.completion","created":1784820757,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    import heapq\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\\n    removed = set()\\n    heap = []\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    visit_order = []\\n    while heap:\\n        neg_depth, nid = heapq.heappop(heap)\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        visit_order.append(nid)\\n        node = node_map[nid]\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        is_leaf = True\\n        for cid in children.get(nid, []):\\n            if cid not in removed:\\n                is_leaf = False\\n                break\\n        if not is_leaf:\\n            continue\\n        if node['utility'] <= threshold:\\n            removed.add(nid)\\n            parent_id = node['parent']\\n            if parent_id is not None and parent_id not in removed:\\n                parent_node = node_map[parent_id]\\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (-parent_node['depth'], parent_id))\\n    retained_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed]\\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'visit_order': visit_order}\"}","reasoning_content":"We are given a task: implement `solve(case)` that processes a tree with nodes having fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. There's a utility_threshold. The objective: \"Maintain and update the syntax-guided reduction work queue across accepted and rejected tree rewrites without revisiting invalidated nodes.\" Essentially, we need to simulate a process: we have a tree, we need to remove removable leaf nodes whose utility <= threshold. After removal, we need to consider the parent again (enqueue it) because it might become a leaf and might be removable. We must not revisit nodes that are invalidated (i.e., removed). We need to return \"retained IDs and the complete visit order\". The output should be a JSON-serializable object. Likely we need to return a dict with keys like \"retained_ids\" and \"visit_order\" (or similar). Based on the description: \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" So we will return an object with those two lists.\n\nWe need to parse the input case. It has \"nodes\" list and \"utility_threshold\". Build a tree from nodes. The root has parent null. We'll store each node as a dict with its fields. We'll need to track children for each node.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Build a dict of nodes by id.\n- Build children list for each node.\n- Initialize a queue: all nodes (excluding root?) sorted by descending depth, and within same depth by node ID stable order (alphanumeric? based on id strings). Since depth is given, we can sort by (-depth, id). The queue should include all nodes? But we only need to process removable leaves initially? However the description says \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order.\" That suggests we start with all nodes in that order. But then we \"Skip nodes already removed.\" So we can start with all nodes? But we also need to process removal only for removable leaves with low utility. So we will iterate through the queue, but we need to handle enqueuing parent after removal. Also we must not revisit invalidated (removed) nodes.\n\nWe need to keep track of which nodes are removed (initially none). Also we need to keep a set of removed ids.\n\nWe'll have a visit_order list to record the order in which we \"visit\" nodes (i.e., when we consider them in the queue). The description says: \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" So we need to output the list of IDs that are retained (i.e., not removed) after processing, and the order in which nodes were visited (including root? likely all nodes that were ever considered? But we might need to include only those that we actually processed? The phrase \"complete visit order\" suggests we record each time we pop from queue and consider the node, even if skipped (because removed or not removable leaf). But careful: \"Skip nodes already removed.\" So if we pop a node that is removed, we don't count it as visit? Probably not. The visit order should include only nodes that we actually evaluate for removal? Or all nodes that we process? I'd assume we record when we pop from queue and consider it, and if it is not removed, we check if it's a removable leaf with utility <= threshold. If so, we remove it, enqueue its parent (if parent exists and is not removed). The node being removed is still visited. So we record the id in visit_order when we pop it and it's not already removed. If it's already removed, we skip (don't visit)? That's ambiguous. The description: \"Skip nodes already removed.\" That suggests we just ignore them, not include them in visit order. So visit order only includes nodes that were not removed when popped.\n\nAlso, after removal, we enqueue its retained parent. The parent may already be in the queue? But we need to ensure it's considered again. So we add it to the queue. But to avoid duplicates, we might need to track if the parent is already enqueued? The queue order should be descending depth and stable ID. Since we are dynamically adding nodes, we need to maintain that order. We can just append to a list and then sort each time? That would be inefficient but fine for small cases. Or we can use a priority queue. But we need to maintain order: first by depth descending, then by id ascending. So we can use a min-heap with negative depth? Actually descending depth so we want highest depth first. So we can push (-depth, id) into a heap. But we also need stable ordering for same depth by id ascending, so that works: (-depth, id) where id is string, Python compares strings lexicographically. That ensures that for same depth, lower id string comes first. So we can use heapq.\n\nInitialize heap with all nodes that are not the root? The root is not removable (removable: false). It might still be visited? The queue should include all nodes? But root depth 0, so it will be at the end. And root is not removable, so it won't be removed. But we still need to consider it? Possibly, because after removals, root might become a leaf? But root has no parent, and we never remove root. So we could include root in initial queue, but it will never be removed. It might be visited and then nothing happens. But it's okay. However the description says \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order.\" So we should include all nodes. But we need to be careful: \"Skip nodes already removed.\" So root won't be removed, fine.\n\nBut also, are we supposed to remove only leaf nodes? The description: \"Remove only a removable leaf whose utility is at most the threshold.\" So we only remove nodes that are leaves (i.e., have no children that are not removed? Actually after removals, children might be removed, so a node becomes leaf). So we need to check if a node is leaf: that it has no children in the current tree (i.e., no child that is not removed). So we need to keep a set of removed ids, and when checking if a node is leaf, we look at its children list and filter out removed ones.\n\nThus algorithm:\n\n- Parse nodes into dict node_map.\n- Build children list for each node: for each node, if parent not null, add node id to parent's children list.\n- Initialize removed set empty.\n- Initialize heap with all node ids: for each node id, push (-node.depth, node.id). Since depth is given, we can use that.\n- Initialize visit_order list empty.\n- While heap not empty:\n    - pop (neg_depth, node_id) from heap.\n    - if node_id in removed: continue (skip already removed)\n    - add node_id to visit_order.\n    - node = node_map[node_id]\n    - if not node['removable']: continue (skip non-removable, but still visited)\n    - Check if leaf: get children from node_map? Actually we need to check children of this node. We have children list built. We need to check if any child is not removed. So leaf condition: all children are removed or node has no children initially. So we can check: for each child_id in children[node_id], if child_id not in removed -> not leaf. If no such child, then leaf.\n    - If leaf and node['utility'] <= utility_threshold:\n        - remove node: add node_id to removed set.\n        - enqueue parent: if node['parent'] is not None and node['parent'] not in removed:\n            - push (-depth_of_parent, parent_id) into heap. Note: depth_of_parent from node_map[node['parent']]['depth'].\n        - Note: We do not need to remove children because they are already removed? Actually if node becomes leaf, it means its children are already removed (or never had children). But we are removing the leaf itself. That's fine.\n    - Continue loop.\n\nAfter loop, we have removed set. Retained IDs are all node ids not in removed. The visit_order is the list of nodes visited (in order popped from heap, not including skipped removed nodes). We need to return both lists.\n\nOne nuance: The queue order must be descending depth and stable node-ID. Using heap with (-depth, id) works because we pop smallest (-depth) first, which is largest depth. For same depth, id string comparison ensures stable ordering. But when we enqueue parent, we must ensure it is inserted in correct order. Since we push onto heap, it will be popped when its turn comes based on its depth and id. That is fine.\n\nWe also need to consider that after removal, the parent might become a leaf. So we enqueue it for reconsideration.\n\nNow, output: We need to return a JSON-serializable object. Based on the instruction: \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" So likely we return a dict like {\"retained_ids\": [...], \"visit_order\": [...]}. But we need to ensure that the output matches expected format. Since no explicit output format is given, we should use those keys.\n\nAlso, we need to validate every required field before computing a result. The input case has nodes with fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility, and utility_threshold. We can assume they are present. But we might add validation: e.g., ensure nodes list is not empty, ensure id is string, etc. But for simplicity, we can just use them.\n\nWe must not mutate case, and only use stdlib.\n\nWrite the Python code inside a function `solve(case)` that returns the result.\n\nEdge cases: root is not removable, but it might become a leaf? Initially root has children, but after removals, all children might be removed, making root a leaf. But root is not removable, so we don't remove it. But we should still consider it? It will be visited eventually. But we skip non-removable. That's fine.\n\nAlso, we need to handle cases where a node is not removable but is leaf? That's fine, we skip.\n\nNow, write the code.\n\nMake sure to import heapq.\n\nI'll produce a clean implementation.\n\nImplementation steps in code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\n    removed = set()\n    heap = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    visit_order = []\n    while heap:\n        neg_depth, nid = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if nid in removed:\n            continue\n        visit_order.append(nid)\n        node = node_map[nid]\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        # check leaf: no non-removed children\n        is_leaf = True\n        for cid in children.get(nid, []):\n            if cid not in removed:\n                is_leaf = False\n                break\n        if not is_leaf:\n            continue\n        if node['utility'] <= threshold:\n            removed.add(nid)\n            parent_id = node['parent']\n            if parent_id is not None and parent_id not in removed:\n                parent_node = node_map[parent_id]\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (-parent_node['depth'], parent_id))\n    retained_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed]\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'visit_order': visit_order}\n\nTest with given cases? Not needed.\n\nNow we need to format as JSON string in the response. The entire response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\" whose value is the Python source code as a JSON string (escaped). So we need to output something like {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import heapq\\n    ...\"} but ensure newlines are escaped as \\n, quotes escaped, etc.\n\nWe can generate the JSON string by using json.dumps on the Python source. But since we are writing manually, we need to ensure proper escaping.\n\nBetter to produce the JSON manually: write the code as a string with proper escapes.\n\nI'll write the code with proper indentation and then escape it.\n\nGiven the requirement: \"Encode the Python source as a JSON string, escaping newlines, backslashes, and quotes as required by JSON.\"\n\nSo I'll take the source code, replace newline with \\n, backslash with \\\\, double quote with \\\", etc.\n\nI'll do it step by step.\n\nFirst, the source code (without import at top? Use import inside function or top-level? We can import at top of solve function. But it's allowed to use only standard library, so importing heapq inside solve is fine. But we could also import at module level, but since we are defining a function, we can have imports inside the function. To be safe, I'll include import heapq inside the function.\n\nWrite the function:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    import heapq\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\n    removed = set()\n    heap = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    visit_order = []\n    while heap:\n        neg_depth, nid = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if nid in removed:\n            continue\n        visit_order.append(nid)\n        node = node_map[nid]\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        is_leaf = True\n        for cid in children.get(nid, []):\n            if cid not in removed:\n                is_leaf = False\n                break\n        if not is_leaf:\n            continue\n        if node['utility'] <= threshold:\n            removed.add(nid)\n            parent_id = node['parent']\n            if parent_id is not None and parent_id not in removed:\n                parent_node = node_map[parent_id]\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (-parent_node['depth'], parent_id))\n    retained_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed]\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'visit_order': visit_order}\n\nNow escape for JSON.\n\nWe need to produce a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value that string.\n\nI'll manually escape. For readability, I'll use triple-quoted string? No, must be JSON.\n\nBetter to use a Python script to generate, but here I'll do manually.\n\nLet me write the source as a single line with \\n.\n\nStart: def solve(case):\\n    import heapq\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\\n  